---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import SignatureMaker from '../components/SignatureMaker.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Signature Maker: Draw or Type a Signature Online | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Draw your signature with a mouse or finger, or type it in a handwriting font, then save it as a transparent PNG or a white JPG and resize it to a size in KB.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: signature maker', '/signature-maker/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/signature-maker/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero badge="Free · Your signature stays on this device · No sign-up" title="Make a signature" lead="Draw your signature or type it in a handwriting style, save it as a transparent PNG or a white JPG, and shrink it to the size an upload form asks for. Nothing is uploaded." />

  <SignatureMaker client:load />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      The signature maker runs inside your browser, so it will not work while JavaScript is turned off. Enable
      JavaScript and reload to use it. The notes below explain which kind of signature suits an upload form.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>Drawn or typed</h2>
    <p>
      A drawn signature is your own hand and is the safest choice when a form asks for "a scan of your signature". It
      works with a mouse, a trackpad, a stylus or a finger. A typed signature is made from your name in a handwriting
      font. It is quick and tidy, and it is the option to use if you cannot draw on your device, but some portals and
      employers want a hand-signed image and reject anything that looks typed. Check the form's wording before you
      choose.
    </p>

    <h2>PNG or JPG</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>PNG, transparent.</strong> Only your ink is saved, with nothing behind it, so you can place it on a document or a form. Many upload forms do not accept PNG.</li>
      <li><strong>JPG, white.</strong> Your ink on a plain white background. This is the format most upload forms ask for, and it is the one the resize step uses.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Getting it under a size limit</h2>
    <p>
      Forms often limit a signature to a small size such as 20 KB. Choose "Resize to KB" and the signature you just made
      opens in the signature resizer further down this page, with no file to pick. Set the limit, and the tool lowers
      the quality and then the pixel size until the file fits. A signature is mostly white space, so it usually shrinks
      well, but a very small limit on a large picture can make thin strokes look rough.
    </p>

    <h2>Tips for a clean result</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sign slowly and fill the box. A larger signature stays readable after it is shrunk.</li>
      <li>Use black or dark blue. Pale ink can fail a form's contrast check.</li>
      <li>Undo removes the last stroke, so you can fix one slip without starting over.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Will a typed signature be accepted?</h3>
    <p>
      It depends on the portal. Some accept any image of a signature and some ask for a hand-signed one. This page
      cannot know which, so if the form says "handwritten" or "scanned", draw it or sign on paper and use the <a href="/resize-signature/">signature resizer</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>Are the fonts free to use?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes. Caveat and Dancing Script are published under the SIL Open Font License and Homemade Apple under the Apache
      License 2.0. They are served from this site, not from an outside font service.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my signature uploaded or saved?</h3>
    <p>
      No. It is drawn and saved in your browser and is gone when you close or reload the page. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/signature-maker/" />
</Base>
